{"ts":1781440816,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(plugin_mailbox_no_reentrant_host_fn, architecture, 'No host function exposed to WASM guests may invoke a path that reaches PluginHostPort::on_file_indexed/on_file_changed: the worker thread is the single consumer of its own bounded mailbox, so re-entering fan_out from inside a hook handler would block the worker on a send to its own full mailbox and self-deadlock. Today this holds only because all host functions (host_read_file, host_list_files, ast_store_get/put, host_log) are read-only or non-reentrant; adding any file-mutating host function reintroduces the hazard.', high, 'feat/internal-plugin-queue design+review 2026-06')"}
{"ts":1781440817,"op":"assert","clause":"trigger(plugin_mailbox_no_reentrant_host_fn, 'crates/core_engine/src/adapters/plugin/loader.rs', directory)"}
{"ts":1781440820,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(plugin_registry_no_write_on_serving_path, architecture, 'No MCP-serving code path may take a write() guard on the Arc<RwLock<PluginHostRegistry>>. Watcher hook delivery (SharedPluginHost::on_file_*) does a blocking backpressuring mailbox send while holding the registry RwLock read guard; this never starves MCP only because every hot-path consumer (declared_tools, call_instance_tool, hooks) is also a reader and RwLock permits concurrent readers. A write() on a serving path would be blocked by watcher backpressure. register() is the only writer and runs at startup before the Arc<RwLock> is shared.', high, 'feat/internal-plugin-queue design+review 2026-06')"}
{"ts":1781440821,"op":"assert","clause":"trigger(plugin_registry_no_write_on_serving_path, 'crates/core_engine/src/domain/plugin_host', directory)"}
{"ts":1781444546,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(mcp_mutations_use_real_plugin_host, architecture, 'MCP file-mutation handlers (create/delete/global_replace in adapters/mcp/native_tools.rs) MUST construct FileMutationService with the real plugin host, not NoOpPluginHost. The filesystem watcher cannot compensate: the MCP handler already mutated the shared VFS, so the watcher event hits an idempotent early-return (handle_create/handle_delete) and never fires the hook. Symptom when violated: deleted file symbols persist in the AST index forever (index-as-you-go cannot self-heal a deletion).', high, 'feat/internal-plugin-queue BUG1 fix a80f205 2026-06')"}
{"ts":1781444549,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(reconcile_persisted_index_on_startup, architecture, 'On normal boot (scan already complete) the persisted sled (workspace,index) is loaded verbatim; it MUST be reconciled against the current filesystem or stale entries become permanent ghosts (files deleted while the server was down, indexed by older code, or foreign atomic-save temps). Fix in scan::reconcile_pruned: path-only ignore-aware walk, drop tracked entries absent from the walk. Do NOT re-read/hash file contents (perf on large repos). Empirically: wiping .tower/db and rebuilding removed all ghosts, confirming load-verbatim as the cause.', high, 'feat/internal-plugin-queue BUG2 fix d76604d 2026-06')"}
{"ts":1781700896,"op":"assert","clause":"rule(bench_fsync_use_real_disk, pitfall, 'When benchmarking fsync or write durability, do NOT use the default tempdir: /tmp is tmpfs (RAM) so fsync is near-free and numbers are ~100x too fast. Force TMPDIR onto a real disk (e.g. under $HOME on NVMe/btrfs) before measuring', high, f3_ast_persist_benchmark)"}
{"ts":1781700896,"op":"assert","clause":"trigger(bench_fsync_use_real_disk, 'benchmark', project)"}
